   <dc:creator>
    <rdf:Seq>
     <rdf:li>Yousef Al-Zalzalah</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Exact Beta-P Margins for Dependent Competing Risks: Partial Identification and Sensitivity Bounds</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Exact Beta-P Margins for Dependent Competing Risks: Partial Identification and Sensitivity Bounds</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>shared gamma frailty; latent failure times; active-risk functionals; profile likelihood; cumulative incidence</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Competing-risks data identify the earliest failure time and its cause</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Competing-risks data identify the earliest failure time and its cause</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but generally do not identify the dependence among latent failure times. We construct a shared-gamma frailty model that preserves distinct Beta-P marginal survival distributions exactly. Within this model</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but generally do not identify the dependence among latent failure times. We construct a shared-gamma frailty model that preserves distinct Beta-P marginal survival distributions exactly. Within this model</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">we derive the joint survival function</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">we derive the joint survival function</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">cause-specific subdensities</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">cause-specific subdensities</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and net</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and net</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">crude</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">crude</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and partial-crude risk functionals. We establish positive upper-orthant dependence and an ordering result for nested active-risk sets. We also define model-specific structural sensitivity measures and likelihood-supported probability regions. Using a publicly available transplantation dataset (n = 177)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and partial-crude risk functionals. We establish positive upper-orthant dependence and an ordering result for nested active-risk sets. We also define model-specific structural sensitivity measures and likelihood-supported probability regions. Using a publicly available transplantation dataset (n = 177)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">we compare the Aalen--Johansen estimates with the independent Beta-P boundary model and examine dependence through a multi-start profile analysis that re-estimates the marginal parameters at each of 25 grid values. The best likelihood found occurs at the independence boundary (log-likelihood approximately -561.2107); the numerical search found no improvement for positive dependence. We report descriptive profile-grid probability ranges at 12 and 24 months and separate fixed-margin sensitivity ranges. Seeded Monte Carlo studies check the probability calculations and the complete estimator. The formula check has a maximum absolute discrepancy of 0.0016; a separate 300-replicate study obtains reproducible constrained optimization but reveals substantial boundary selection and weak complete-estimator identification at n = 177. The profile-grid ranges are neither confidence intervals nor certified sharp bounds. The model illustrates how assumptions about latent dependence affect latent and observable risk functionals; it does not demonstrate improved clinical prediction.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">we compare the Aalen--Johansen estimates with the independent Beta-P boundary model and examine dependence through a multi-start profile analysis that re-estimates the marginal parameters at each of 25 grid values. The best likelihood found occurs at the independence boundary (log-likelihood approximately -561.2107); the numerical search found no improvement for positive dependence. We report descriptive profile-grid probability ranges at 12 and 24 months and separate fixed-margin sensitivity ranges. Seeded Monte Carlo studies check the probability calculations and the complete estimator. The formula check has a maximum absolute discrepancy of 0.0016; a separate 300-replicate study obtains reproducible constrained optimization but reveals substantial boundary selection and weak complete-estimator identification at n = 177. The profile-grid ranges are neither confidence intervals nor certified sharp bounds. The model illustrates how assumptions about latent dependence affect latent and observable risk functionals; it does not demonstrate improved clinical prediction.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
